WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.000
CASSIE KOZYRKOV:
So, have any of you

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.820
had that moment
where you realize

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.640
everyone is using
some word, and you

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.130
have no idea what that
means but you're too

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.040
afraid to ask at this point?

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.940
What even is machine learning?

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.270
Well, it's an approach to
making lots of small decisions

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.540
with data, something involving
algorithmically finding

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.380
patterns in data.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.220
But let me just put it bluntly,
so that we can all get it.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.207
It's a thing
labeler, essentially.

00:00:38.207 --> 00:00:39.540
That's what machine learning is.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.470
It's just a thing labeler.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:48.570
So why on earth should you be
excited about thing labeling?

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:51.570
Well, here's my pet,
Huxley, and you've just

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.650
taken in some pretty complicated
data through your senses,

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.920
and you just know
that that's a cat.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.890
And if I show you a different
image, you're not fooled--

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.040
you still know that
that label is cat.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.530
Your brain just does this.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.590
You don't even know
how your brain does it.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.570
Now, if we wanted
to get a computer

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.390
to perform this
labeling task for us,

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.130
if we went the traditional way--

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.170
you know this,
programmers-- we would

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.630
communicate with the
universe in some way,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.540
think really deeply
about the problem,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.079
and come up with a model.

00:01:26.079 --> 00:01:26.620
It's a model.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.180
It's just a recipe.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.840
It's a set of instructions
that the computer

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.860
has to follow to get from
the image to the label.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.550
And we would have
to hand-craft that.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.770
Now, think about what
this recipe would

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.850
have to be for the computer
to correctly detect that there

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.180
is a cat in an image.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.760
I mean, think about
what you, your brain,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.680
actually did with those pixels.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.180
Can you express that?

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.820
Do you know what
your brain is doing?

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.740
Or have you just had the
benefit of eons of evolution,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:02.420
and it just kind of
figures it out for you?

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.680
Now, that recipe is
really hard to hand-craft.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.509
Wouldn't it be better if you
could just say to the machine,

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:11.310
here, look at a bunch
of examples of cats,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.380
look at a bunch of
examples of not cats,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.040
and just figure it out yourself.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.290
That is what machine
learning is all about.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.970
It is a completely different
programming paradigm.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.570
Now instead of giving
explicit instructions, you

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.900
program with examples,
with data, and let

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.480
that machine learning
algorithm figure it out

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.280
and stitch that code that
goes in between for you.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.080
So why should you actually be
excited by this thing labeling?

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.240
Well, engineers,
you know we like

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.980
to get computers
to do stuff for us?

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:52.050
There's a whole class of tasks
where we just cannot express

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.540
the instructions.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.220
They're ineffable.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:04.410
AI and machine learning are
about automating the ineffable.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:07.830
This is a fundamental
leap in human progress.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.540
Now you can get computers to do
tasks where you cannot possibly

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.560
express the instructions.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.711
This is so exciting, and seems
futuristic and difficult,

00:03:20.711 --> 00:03:21.210
right?

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.720
And when you think
about machine learning,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:37.098
the one thing I most want you
to think about is microwaves.

00:03:37.098 --> 00:03:39.360
Who here in the
audience knows how

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.900
a microwave oven works
well enough to build

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:43.950
a new one from scratch?

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.380
Raise hands.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.380
Who can build a new one?

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.460
Do I see any takers?

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.460
Any takers?

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:50.160
No hands.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.740
Yeah, me neither.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.970
Who here has never used
one to reheat food?

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.050
You all have.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.690
Even though you have
no idea how it works,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.300
you're still happy
to use one, right?

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.230
And anyone here feel like
you would have no idea how

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.150
to get hold of one, I
don't know, like maybe

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:13.120
at Kohl's, if you needed one?

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.150
Now, you just told me
you have no idea how

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:20.339
it works, so how could you
possibly trust this microwave?

00:04:20.339 --> 00:04:22.710
Well, you're not going to
trust it by reading the wiring

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:23.945
diagram, are you?

00:04:23.945 --> 00:04:26.550
You're going to trust it by
checking that it actually

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.640
does work, by having a
good idea of what you want

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.110
it to do for you, and then
tasting what comes out

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.520
on the other side.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.010
And that is exactly what you'll
do with machine learning.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.830
And this is a perfect
analogy for machine learning.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.530
Now, what they don't tell
you about machine learning--

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.080
when we use that
term, there's actually

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.570
two machine learnings, not one.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.250
Two completely
different disciplines

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.560
that are as different
as building microwaves

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.060
from scratch and
innovating in the kitchen,

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.200
and you need a completely
different skill sets for these.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.140
If you want to be a machine
learning researcher,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:09.340
you are building general purpose
tools for other people to use.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:13.140
And so the bad news there
is that there is quite

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:15.210
a lot of education
that you need to get,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.610
takes a little while to ramp
up, because how on earth

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:20.068
are you going to build a better
microwave than the one that

00:05:20.068 --> 00:05:21.110
exists already--

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.610
there's some pretty
sophisticated stuff out there--

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.342
if you have no idea how
the current one works?

00:05:26.342 --> 00:05:27.300
So that's the bad news.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.390
It takes a long time to become
a machine learning researcher.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:33.510
The good news, though, is
if that is your cup of tea,

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:37.500
we at Google have a large
machining research shop

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.110
where we make these
tools for you that

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.300
are all powered by open source.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.420
So we give you those wiring
diagrams as a springboard

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.660
so that you can go and build
a better microwave than what

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.600
we have using our blueprints.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.380
And TensorFlow, a really popular
machine learning project,

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.780
started at Google before
we open sourced it

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.090
because we believe that
the community as a whole

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:06.720
is so much more innovative
than just any one of us alone.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.740
But let's face it--

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.970
most of you are not here
to build general purpose

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.320
microwaves.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.790
You just want to get
cooking already, right?

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:18.210
You just want to use these
things to solve business

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.230
problems, and so you are in the
completely other discipline--

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:25.200
applied machine learning.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.440
Now, for that, you
do not need a PhD.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.540
You don't need to know
how back propagation works

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.420
in neural networks any more
than a chef needs to know

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.400
how a microwave is wired.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.520
Instead, what you need is a
really good kitchen to play in,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.730
and we at Google
provide that for you.

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.205
Let me show you some of
the things that we have,

00:06:47.205 --> 00:06:49.080
and what you're going
to need kind of depends

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.120
on what you're cooking.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.850
If you're cooking with
usual ingredients-- data--

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.550
and you're making usual dishes--

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.170
labels, predictions.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.020
So say you're making pizza.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.310
Please don't go reinvent the
concept of pizza from scratch.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.500
There's already
recipes out there,

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.420
and you can just grab those
recipes and start making pizza.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:17.520
And that is what our
cloud APIs are all about.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.490
These are recipes that we've
already built, that you can

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:23.280
just pick up and start using.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.560
You can just plug
them into your apps

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.600
to make those
intelligent applications.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:33.120
But say you're cooking
with a little twist.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.530
Now it's gluten
free, vegan pizza.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.630
Well, you still
don't need to-- yeah.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.820
You still don't need to
start entirely from scratch.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:43.980
It's still pizza.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:48.590
You can just adjust what
already exists out there.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:53.970
And so what we suggest for
you here is try Cloud AutoML.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:58.830
Sure, maybe we haven't
trained our vision system

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:06.330
on the clothing type that your
Kohl's customers are wearing.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:10.580
But no problem-- just feed in
your images with your labels

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:14.520
into Cloud AutoML and
you'll be good to go.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.980
Only a small
adjustment necessary.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.160
Now, say you're
truly innovating.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.690
You have your own completely
unique ingredients.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:28.980
You're doing something pretty
different in the kitchen.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:31.460
I don't know, maybe now
you're making an edible sock

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:34.740
that tastes like pizza.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:38.789
Well, machine learning
and innovation

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:40.840
are in Google's core DNA.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:44.010
In fact, I can't think of a
single one of our consumer

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.170
products that
doesn't have machine

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.850
learning in it somewhere.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:51.760
And so if you are
innovating in the kitchen,

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:58.230
if you're building truly new
recipes, what we have for you

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.200
is Cloud Machine
Learning Engine.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.000
This is access to exactly the
same infrastructure that Google

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.850
uses to train our models.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:09.400
Incredible.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.210
So you could, if you wanted
to, after this session,

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:15.690
grab a laptop, open
it up, and test

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.570
drive our shiny,
gleaming kitchen

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.790
full of those
appliances ready to go.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.800
You could feel the
thrill of a data center

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.190
and all those cutting
edge algorithms rising up

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:27.340
to do your bidding.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:33.040
So solving business problems
with machine learning--

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.470
applied machine
learning-- is far easier

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:37.270
than what most people think.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:38.860
What you don't
realize is that all

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.410
those courses in university--

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:44.230
those are all about how to
build the microwave, not how

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:46.690
to just use it to get cooking.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:49.420
And when I hear people say,
oh, I couldn't possibly

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:52.420
get started with machine
learning until I take

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:57.160
a course in it, or,
goodness, a whole degree,

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:00.070
I can't help but imagine
that same person saying,

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:01.550
I couldn't possibly
use a microwave

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:04.720
until I've built one myself.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.260
You can just get cooking.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:08.380
It's not the lack of
degree, or course,

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:12.230
or knowledge that's
holding you back.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.510
What's much, much more
important than that,

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:20.530
what you need to get
started is creativity.

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:24.010
Figure out what you're going
to cook because all those tools

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:27.670
are already there waiting
for you to use them.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:30.850
Imagine gleaming
kitchens just waiting

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.920
for you to come play in them.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.690
But since this is
a thing labeler,

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:36.310
you need to start
by thinking, what

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.010
on earth do you want labeled?

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:42.130
And see how creative
you can get with that.

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:47.350
You might imagine the label
being maybe the wave form that

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:52.180
sounds like a voice that
could make a hairdresser's

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:53.050
appointment for you.

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:56.140
Or you could imagine
a few other cases,

00:10:56.140 --> 00:11:01.660
like maybe you are a loving
son, and your aging parents sort

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:05.140
vegetables by hand, and
you want to save them

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:07.900
the trouble of having to
do that on their farm.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:10.510
So maybe you're thinking
about building a system that

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:15.840
will label the vegetables
and sort it automatically

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.567
so your parents
don't have to do it.

00:11:17.567 --> 00:11:19.650
Or maybe you're worried
about the ingredients that

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.280
go into baby food, and you
think it would be great

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.450
if you could automatically check
whether the ingredient is safe

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:29.880
or spoiled before it goes in.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:34.110
Or maybe you're into
the idea of a huge bank

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:39.950
with trillions of dollars
in assets countering fraud

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:43.400
and money laundering
automatically

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:48.320
across millions of accounts.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:52.360
If you imagined that world,
you imagined this world.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.350
Well, these are
all real examples

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:57.350
of things that have been done
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:57.350 --> 00:12:00.710
The first is a real family
of cucumber farmers,

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.860
the second is a Japanese
manufacturer of high quality

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:07.580
baby food products,
Kewpie, and the third

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:13.340
is HSBC, who uses Google
Cloud to run a better business

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:18.200
and catch the bad guys
across 27 million customers.

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:23.720
Pretty different chefs,
completely different

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.220
applications.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:29.660
Why is Google Cloud Platform the
right choice for all of them?

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:32.690
Well, think about what you
might want in a kitchen.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:34.850
You'd want good engineering.

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:37.820
So you probably want to think
about getting a partner whose

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:40.970
quality you trust.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:43.250
But think more
long-term than that.

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:47.320
If you believe in your
own infinite creativity,

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:48.950
if you believe in
the future, you

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:54.200
would be crazy to let yourself
get locked into a kitchen

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.860
where the purveyor sets
it up one time for you

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.830
and then controls what you
can and can't have in there,

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:03.970
and every appliance has its
own proprietary little socket

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.350
that you are not
allowed to tamper with.

00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:08.060
And if your business
changes, then you

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:11.760
have to rebuild and
rewire the whole thing.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:15.020
Imagine, instead, the
dream kitchen that

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:20.170
becomes what you need
it to be instantly.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:26.470
Complete, universal
wiring, open interfaces,

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:29.710
the best of everything,
all the modern appliances

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:34.270
always available, and you
can plug them in with no fuss

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.430
because that wiring's universal.

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:39.940
That, my friends, is the
power of open source,

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:44.600
and that is what Google and
Red Hat together believe in.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:47.650
We don't want to see your
creativity squashed by what

00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:51.340
seemed like a good
idea yesterday.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:56.560
So we have built for you an
incredible future-proof machine

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.290
learning kitchen.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:04.480
This kitchen can expand,
contract as you need it.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.400
You can plug in
whatever you like.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:10.210
All the most modern appliances
are there and compatible

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:14.640
as you need them,
fully customizable.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:19.050
Because none of us can
tell what innovations

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.510
the future will
bring, and we don't

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:25.920
want you to have to bet on the
direction of progress and lock

